<!--
    GH-468 follow-up: a loop that only reads memory must not print a store
    into the aliased stack slot it was entered from.  The three sum
    functions fill a 64-byte stack buffer through a call and then walk it.

    sum_gcc and sum_clang are canary_check from ab_test.c built with gcc 11
    and clang-12 at -O2 -fno-stack-protector on x86-64, byte for byte at
    their link addresses (the strncpy import is a stub that writes [rdi]).
    The gcc loop:

        movzbl (%rsp),%eax         ; buf[0], straight from the frame
        test   %al,%al
        je     zero
        lea    0x1(%rsp),%rdx
        xor    %r8d,%r8d
      loop:
        add    $0x1,%rdx
        add    %eax,%r8d
        movzbl -0x1(%rdx),%eax     ; next byte, through the pointer
        test   %al,%al
        jne    loop

    sum_msvc is the MSVC /O2 loop from ab_o2.exe main (0x1400012a3), the
    same instructions with the call's arguments in rdi/rsi/edx: the guard
    reads buf[0] with `cmp byte [rsp+0x20],dl` and the latch reloads
    buf[i] with `cmp byte [rsp+rax+0x20],0`.

    In all three the loop variable is MULTIEQUAL(buf[0], LOAD) at the loop
    head.  Stage: P6 forced merge (Merge::mergeOp).  P0 assertion: option
    tiedphitrim on|off.  With the option off, Merge puts the buf[0] read and
    the LOAD into one HighVariable, which is the frame slot, so every byte
    the loop fetches prints as a store into buf[0].  With the option on (the
    default, pass 1) the loop-head input is trimmed into a copy on the
    entering edge and the loop variable is its own.

    Negative controls, identical in both passes:
    - store_loop really stores each fetched byte into buf[0] inside the loop
      and passes buf to a call after it, so the store is live and must print;
      the write into buf[0] inside the loop declines the trim.
    - store_join stores the joined value into buf[0] after an if, not a loop,
      and passes buf on.
    - outparam_join fills an int local through a call and joins it with a
      LOAD at an if/else, not at a loop head.
    - accum_loop fills an int local through a call and enters a loop whose
      value is computed from it, not read from memory.
    - xa11 and xa13 (gcc 11 -O2, byte for byte) enter a byte loop from buf[0]
      and reload it through a LOAD, but also compute x in the loop and store
      it into buf[0] after the loop.  The trim would let x merge into buf[0],
      printing a store inside the loop that the loop's first reload reads
      back, so the loop keeps upstream's merge.

    t8, t1 and t1b (gcc 11 -O2, byte for byte; their callees are stubs and
    each group sits at its link addresses plus a constant, so the rel32 calls
    are unchanged) walk buf from buf[0] and store the loop's exit byte into a
    neighbouring slot, buf[1] or buf[2], after the loop; the loop's first
    reload reads that slot's original byte.  The trimmed loop variable must not
    merge into that neighbouring slot either, or the store would print before
    the loop and the reload would read it back.

    outparam_walk (gcc 11 -O2, byte for byte) walks a list whose head a call
    fills into a stack slot.  The default pass copies the head into its own
    variable; only with the option off does each next pointer print as a store
    into the slot.  It fails if Merge folds the loop variable back into the
    slot after the trim.
-->
